WEBVTT

1
00:00:01.200 --> 00:00:09.798
This video explains the optical circuit
switch, or OCS, and how the OCS cables connect

2
00:00:09.798 --> 00:00:11.397
at the TPU rack.

3
00:00:12.747 --> 00:00:20.437
We will cover what an optical circuit switch
does, the SPOCS racks, the OCS cable itself,

4
00:00:20.437 --> 00:00:28.299
what each color means, how to plug in at the
OCS panel, and how to spot a wrong connection.

5
00:00:32.449 --> 00:00:35.948
OCS stands for Optical Circuit Switch.

6
00:00:36.698 --> 00:00:41.775
The guide describes it as a shelf that steers
light between fibers.

7
00:00:43.275 --> 00:00:48.630
Light comes in on one fiber, and the switch
points it out on another fiber.

8
00:00:49.380 --> 00:00:55.936
Change the setting, and the same light goes
somewhere else, without anyone touching a

9
00:00:55.936 --> 00:00:56.399
cable.

10
00:00:57.749 --> 00:01:05.459
Public research from Google describes how: the
switch uses tiny movable mirrors to point the

11
00:01:05.459 --> 00:01:05.962
light.

12
00:01:07.312 --> 00:01:14.208
This lets the system link the TPU racks of one
pod together in different shapes, for

13
00:01:14.208 --> 00:01:15.440
different jobs.

14
00:01:16.790 --> 00:01:19.030
Here is why that matters to you.

15
00:01:19.780 --> 00:01:23.876
The switch can only steer light it actually
receives.

16
00:01:24.626 --> 00:01:29.682
If a leg is in the wrong port, that link
simply does not exist.

17
00:01:33.832 --> 00:01:37.245
The OCS shelves live in SPOCS racks.

18
00:01:37.995 --> 00:01:41.451
SPOCS stands for Super Pod OCS rack.

19
00:01:42.201 --> 00:01:45.167
Each one holds four OCS shelves.

20
00:01:47.467 --> 00:01:51.392
Each pod has three SPOCS racks, color coded.

21
00:01:52.142 --> 00:01:54.254
Red is SPOCS rack one.

22
00:01:55.604 --> 00:01:57.929
Orange is SPOCS rack two.

23
00:01:59.279 --> 00:02:01.669
And yellow is SPOCS rack three.

24
00:02:03.019 --> 00:02:10.144
Every TPU rack in the pod sends one OCS cable
to each of these three racks.

25
00:02:10.894 --> 00:02:15.566
One hundred forty-four TPU racks, three cables
each.

26
00:02:19.716 --> 00:02:22.020
This is an OCS cable.

27
00:02:22.770 --> 00:02:27.847
This one has an orange jacket, so it is OCS
cable two.

28
00:02:29.247 --> 00:02:33.877
It has eighteen fibers, and nine LC legs at
each end.

29
00:02:34.627 --> 00:02:41.667
Each leg is an LC duplex, two fibers, with the
angled APC polish.

30
00:02:42.417 --> 00:02:44.742
Nine times two is eighteen.

31
00:02:46.092 --> 00:02:47.799
Eight legs are used.

32
00:02:48.549 --> 00:02:51.749
The ninth leg, the yellow one, is the spare.

33
00:02:53.099 --> 00:02:59.307
The legs are labeled with the number of the
OCS panel port they plug into.

34
00:03:00.057 --> 00:03:04.089
On cable two, the labels are nine through
sixteen.

35
00:03:05.439 --> 00:03:07.849
The cable is called type B.

36
00:03:08.599 --> 00:03:15.816
In each pair, one fiber sends and one
receives, and a type B cable crosses them over

37
00:03:15.816 --> 00:03:18.135
so sending meets receiving.

38
00:03:18.885 --> 00:03:21.531
The cutsheet already accounts for it.

39
00:03:22.281 --> 00:03:24.222
You do not swap anything.

40
00:03:26.522 --> 00:03:29.466
Each cable is cut to length for its rack.

41
00:03:30.216 --> 00:03:36.253
Before you pull it, check the part number and
the length on the reel against the cutsheet.

42
00:03:40.403 --> 00:03:45.993
There are three kinds of color, and a number,
on every OCS cable.

43
00:03:46.743 --> 00:03:48.343
Let us sort them out.

44
00:03:49.543 --> 00:03:55.473
The jacket color tells you which cable it is,
and which SPOCS rack it goes to.

45
00:03:56.223 --> 00:04:00.362
Red goes to one, orange to two, yellow to
three.

46
00:04:01.712 --> 00:04:08.412
The leg colors, blue through black, tell you
each leg's position in its cable, from one to

47
00:04:08.412 --> 00:04:08.859
eight.

48
00:04:10.209 --> 00:04:13.707
A yellow leg is position nine: the spare.

49
00:04:15.057 --> 00:04:21.116
And the number on the leg label is the OCS
panel port it plugs into.

50
00:04:21.866 --> 00:04:26.154
Use the label to pick the port, and the color
as a check.

51
00:04:30.304 --> 00:04:35.893
This is the OCS panel on the TPU rack, called
pp1.

52
00:04:36.643 --> 00:04:41.401
It has twenty-four ports, spread across both
sides of the rack.

53
00:04:42.801 --> 00:04:50.494
OCS cable one, the red one, plugs into the
left group of eight: ports one to eight, on

54
00:04:50.494 --> 00:04:51.121
side A.

55
00:04:52.471 --> 00:04:56.844
OCS cable two, orange, spans both sides.

56
00:04:57.594 --> 00:05:04.037
Ports nine to twelve are on side A, and
thirteen to sixteen are on side B.

57
00:05:05.387 --> 00:05:13.947
OCS cable three, yellow, plugs into the right
group: ports seventeen to twenty-four, on side

58
00:05:13.947 --> 00:05:14.133
B.

59
00:05:15.483 --> 00:05:22.267
In each group, the blue leg goes in the lowest
port, and the black leg goes in the highest.

60
00:05:23.017 --> 00:05:25.620
The yellow spare is not plugged in.

61
00:05:26.970 --> 00:05:34.821
The drawings show the ports on two sides, but
the cutsheet lists them as one panel, pp1.

62
00:05:35.571 --> 00:05:37.469
Go by the port numbers.

63
00:05:41.619 --> 00:05:44.521
Let us plug in OCS cable two.

64
00:05:45.521 --> 00:05:51.238
The orange jacket tells us it is cable two,
going to SPOCS rack two.

65
00:05:51.988 --> 00:05:55.209
In pod ten that is rack 612.

66
00:05:55.959 --> 00:05:59.053
We already checked the reel against the
cutsheet.

67
00:06:00.403 --> 00:06:04.776
On side A: leg nine, blue, goes in port nine.

68
00:06:05.526 --> 00:06:08.278
Leg ten, orange, in port ten.

69
00:06:09.028 --> 00:06:12.185
Then eleven, green, and twelve, brown.

70
00:06:13.535 --> 00:06:18.485
Then on side B: leg thirteen, slate, in port
thirteen.

71
00:06:19.235 --> 00:06:20.771
Fourteen, white.

72
00:06:21.521 --> 00:06:22.907
Fifteen, red.

73
00:06:23.657 --> 00:06:25.385
And sixteen, black.

74
00:06:26.735 --> 00:06:30.298
Each leg is scoped and cleaned before it goes
in.

75
00:06:31.048 --> 00:06:36.360
The bundle gets a wrap-around label, and each
leg gets a flag label.

76
00:06:37.710 --> 00:06:39.310
And the yellow spare?

77
00:06:40.060 --> 00:06:42.300
The guide flags a conflict.

78
00:06:43.050 --> 00:06:49.193
The drawings say to coil it on top of the
rack, but the reference cutsheet says it is

79
00:06:49.193 --> 00:06:50.133
left hanging.

80
00:06:50.883 --> 00:06:55.939
Follow what your site decided, and ask your
lead if you are not sure.

81
00:07:00.089 --> 00:07:01.411
Now you try.

82
00:07:02.161 --> 00:07:03.676
Look at this panel.

83
00:07:04.426 --> 00:07:07.455
The chips show which leg is in each port.

84
00:07:08.205 --> 00:07:09.741
One thing is wrong.

85
00:07:10.491 --> 00:07:12.817
Pause the video and look for it.

86
00:07:16.817 --> 00:07:22.214
Check the color order in each group: blue,
orange, green, brown.

87
00:07:22.964 --> 00:07:25.268
Look at ports nine to twelve.

88
00:07:26.618 --> 00:07:29.114
Ports ten and eleven are swapped.

89
00:07:29.864 --> 00:07:36.285
Leg eleven, green, is in port ten, and leg
ten, orange, is in port eleven.

90
00:07:37.635 --> 00:07:42.030
Why does it matter, if both legs are from the
same cable?

91
00:07:42.780 --> 00:07:48.668
Because each port is a specific link to a
specific shelf at the far end.

92
00:07:49.418 --> 00:07:52.021
A swap connects the wrong fibers.

93
00:07:52.771 --> 00:07:54.179
Swap them back.

94
00:07:58.329 --> 00:08:05.809
In lesson ten of the course, plug OCS legs
into the right ports yourself and find wrong

95
00:08:05.809 --> 00:08:06.841
connections.

96
00:08:07.591 --> 00:08:12.071
Then the next video follows these cables to
the SPOCS racks.

